WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.720
Hey, so what is this? Well,
essentially it's Claude Code

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.160
running on a server rather than
running on your laptop so that

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.568
you can get work done whether
your laptop is with you or not.

00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:14.552
So to start with just why did I
build this for myself initially,

00:00:14.552 --> 00:00:16.792
there were three main things
that I found frustrating.

00:00:16.792 --> 00:00:20.552
The first is that if I didn't have
my laptop and if it wasn't connected

00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:26.792
to WiFi, then I wasn't able to really
get any work done and the agents

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:30.936
certainly the agents that I work with
certainly weren't doing anything.

00:00:30.936 --> 00:00:37.496
Um the second reason is that if I was
uh with my laptop but but the Internet

00:00:37.496 --> 00:00:42.776
connection was kind of poor, so in a
cafe or on a train or on a flight, the

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:47.176
um the agents would just be working
really slowly or it would just be so slow

00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:50.760
that it would be impossible to it was
impossible to get anything done. Um

00:00:50.808 --> 00:00:54.168
And then the third reason is that I
wasn't really able to be productive on my

00:00:54.168 --> 00:01:01.048
phone because I didn't have my full setup
behind it. It was really just a chat.

00:01:01.048 --> 00:01:04.248
Okay, so with that, just wanted to
show you a quick before and after.

00:01:04.248 --> 00:01:07.880
So right now what you're probably doing
is, so this is the Claude Desktop app.

00:01:07.992 --> 00:01:10.552
And what you're probably doing is
starting a new session, picking

00:01:10.552 --> 00:01:14.232
this computer, picking your folder,
and then starting a conversation.

00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:16.392
And then you go back and
forth, and that's how you work.

00:01:16.392 --> 00:01:20.952
And that work runs is running on your
laptop. So that means if you shut

00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:26.808
it down and kind of move somewhere,
the agents stop the work stops.

00:01:26.808 --> 00:01:29.208
by using a server. The only
difference is that rather than

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:33.848
picking this computer, you choose
a a project or a repository

00:01:33.848 --> 00:01:37.128
that's that's on your running
on your server instead.

00:01:37.128 --> 00:01:42.728
So in this case, I'm going to pick OS,
and OS is my sort of second brain project.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.760
And then you just work in the normal
way. So I can paste in a prompt here.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.040
This prompt is just going to summarize
the setup that it has access to.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.560
So what I'm doing here is just
demonstrating that it really is my

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:03.608
full setup. And this is running
on the server. It's not running on

00:02:03.608 --> 00:02:05.224
my my laptop.

00:02:12.408 --> 00:02:15.688
Hopefully I won't take too long. I
picked Sonnet at medium so that it would

00:02:15.688 --> 00:02:22.888
um it be too cool too slow. Okay, cool.
So um here you can see we've got a

00:02:22.888 --> 00:02:28.328
bunch of skills and agents at both the
user level and also within the project.

00:02:28.328 --> 00:02:32.040
A whole load of markdown documents,
so almost a thousand documents.

00:02:32.728 --> 00:02:37.848
And a whole bunch of connected tools
that I use to to get my work done.

00:02:37.848 --> 00:02:42.248
So um with that though, I can now,
with my phone on the right-hand side,

00:02:42.248 --> 00:02:46.088
so it's that the right hand side
is just a mirror of my my iPhone,

00:02:46.088 --> 00:02:51.528
I can open up um Code and this
top conversation here is the exact

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:59.480
same one. So if you see if I scroll up,
you can see that it's exactly the same.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:06.360
And so I can now shut down my laptop if I
wanted to, and start walking and continue

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.480
on on my phone. So I could say, you
know, just for now, I'll just say thanks.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:17.800
And you can see that showed up on the
left-hand side back in the desktop app.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.480
And so I now have my full
setup running from my phone.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:27.080
And I can even open up this
same thing in a browser.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:32.072
So this is a browser logged
into my account, so claude.ai

00:03:32.504 --> 00:03:36.984
in Code. And that same conversation
is on the left-hand side here.

00:03:36.984 --> 00:03:40.984
And as you can see, it has
exactly the same thread.

00:03:40.984 --> 00:03:44.904
So even if I don't have my laptop and
I want to work on a computer, I could

00:03:44.904 --> 00:03:51.144
fire this up somewhere else and work on
someone else's computer if I wanted to.

00:03:51.384 --> 00:03:56.024
So some really quick examples. I have
build work running all the time, sometimes

00:03:56.024 --> 00:04:00.744
through the night, sometimes when I'm in
meetings. And then some workflows that

00:04:00.744 --> 00:04:06.024
I run. So I have a daily workflow. You
could start a daily workflow from your

00:04:06.024 --> 00:04:10.440
laptop before you leave the house. By the
time you get to the train or the tube,

00:04:10.936 --> 00:04:14.296
open it up on your on your phone
and answer the questions in

00:04:14.296 --> 00:04:17.416
order to plan your day. And
then by the time you get to the

00:04:17.416 --> 00:04:22.936
office, your day's ready and you
can kind of start. So that's it.

00:04:22.936 --> 00:04:26.296
Everything runs on your own account
under your own Claude subscription.

00:04:26.296 --> 00:04:30.360
Once the server is built, we just jump
on a quick call to get things finalized.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:36.992
And set and configured. And yeah, there's
more detail on the page play. Cheers.
